The [[UEFA Super Cup]] is an annual [[association football]] match contested between the winners of the [[UEFA Champions League]] and the [[UEFA Europa League]]. Established in 1972, it was contested between the winners of the European Cup (renamed the UEFA Champions League in 1993) and the [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|European/UEFA Cup Winners' Cup]] until 1999, when the latter was discontinued and merged with the UEFA Cup (renamed the Europa League in 2009) by [[UEFA]]. The last Super Cup contested in this format was the [[1999 UEFA Super Cup]] between [[SS Lazio|Lazio]] and [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]], which Lazio won 1–0. The competition was originally played over [[Two-legged tie|two legs]], one at each participating club's stadium in the winter months, but since the [[1998 UEFA Super Cup|1998 edition]], it consists of a single match played at a neutral venue in August.<ref name="format">{{cite web|title=Competition format|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/competitionformat/|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|access-date=28 February 2012}}</ref> Between 1998 and [[2012 UEFA Super Cup|2012]], the [[Stade Louis II]] in [[Monaco]] hosted the Super Cup, but since [[2013 UEFA Super Cup|2013]], it has taken place every year at a different stadium across Europe.<ref>{{cite web|last=Josef|first=Ladislav|title=Prague celebrates 2013 Super Cup honour|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0250-0c50f41ca710-696a44dbb39d-1000--prague-celebrates-2013-super-cup-honour/|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|access-date=31 May 2013|date=17 June 2011}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=UEFA EURO 2020, UEFA Super Cup decisions |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0250-0c50fb8f7dbe-0d15b1a19f6c-1000--uefa-euro-2020-uefa-super-cup-decisions/ |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=31 May 2013 |date=30 June 2012 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130601100042/http://www.uefa.com/uefa/aboutuefa/organisation/executivecommittee/news/newsid%3D1836837.html |archive-date=1 June 2013 }}</ref>

[[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] hold the record for the most victories, having won the competition six times since its inception. [[Paris Saint-Germain]] are the current title holders, having beaten [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]] 2–1 in the [[2026 UEFA Super Cup|2026 edition]].

